[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified both data-plane reachability and the BGP control plane across the fabric. End-to-end, pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) shows 0% loss with ~1 ms RTT in both directions, indicating the data path is healthy at present. On the control plane, every eBGP session is Established and stable. For example, on leaf_router_0_0, neighbors 172.16.0.4 (spine_router_0_0) and 172.16.0.8 (spine_router_0_1) are Established, up ~00:01:57, with “Connections established 1; dropped 0” and each receiving one prefix. On leaf_router_0_1, neighbors 172.16.0.6 and 172.16.0.10 are likewise Established with “established 1; dropped 0.” The spines’ sessions to the super-spine are Established as well, and no neighbor shows resets or drop counts.

Forwarding state matches expectations: leaf_router_0_0 installs B>* 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4, and leaf_router_0_1 installs B>* 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6; spines learn both /24s from the leaves, and super_spine_router_0 has both /24s via 172.16.0.1. Host interfaces and leaf access links show link detected at 10G full-duplex.

Mechanism: Any earlier “intermittent reachability” likely coincided with initial control-plane convergence or ARP warming after the lab started. There is no evidence of current BGP flapping (no drops, stable up/down timers, steady prefixes).

Root cause: No ongoing fault; transient startup convergence rather than a persistent BGP issue.

Corrective fix: No device restart or neighbor clear is warranted. If startup sensitivity is a concern, consider enabling BFD on eBGP sessions or tuning keepalive/hold timers; otherwise, monitor. If users still report intermittent behavior, investigate host ARP/ICMP filtering rather than BGP. 
=== END ===